# Machine-specific settings: credentials, and where the local services listen.
#
# Copy to .env and fill in your own values before the first
# `docker compose up -d`. .env is gitignored and this file is not, so the
# SHAPE of the configuration is version-controlled while its values are not.
# Nothing here is a real credential -- a template that ships a working
# password teaches everyone who clones it to keep using that password.
#
# The split between this file and config/pipeline.yaml is deliberate and has a
# rule: anything that differs between two machines running the same pipeline
# belongs here (ports, hosts, passwords); anything that must be identical for
# the run to mean the same thing belongs in pipeline.yaml (topic names,
# consumer group ids, table names). A teammate changing their Postgres port
# must not be able to change which table the pipeline writes to.

# --- Postgres -------------------------------------------------------------
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING THE FIRST THREE. They are only consulted once, at
# the moment Postgres first starts against an empty pgdata volume: that run
# creates the role, its password and the database, and every start afterwards
# ignores them completely. Editing them later gives you a container whose
# environment says one thing and whose server believes another -- which
# surfaces as `role "..." does not exist` against a container that is running
# perfectly well. Re-running the creation means destroying the volume
# (`docker compose down -v`, which also wipes Kafka), so it is worth getting
# right the first time. Adding a role to an already-initialised server is
# ordinary SQL and needs none of that.
#
# The values below are the fallbacks docker-compose.yml already uses when the
# variable is absent. They are defaults, not requirements -- pick whatever you
# like, as long as .env and the volume were born agreeing.
POSTGRES_USER=pipeline
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME
POSTGRES_DB=transactions

# These two are read by CLIENTS -- the pipeline, the consumer, psql from the
# host -- rather than by the container. Unlike the three above they take
# effect immediately, because nothing was baked from them.
#
# The port is the one on YOUR machine, not inside the container: Postgres
# always serves 5432 in there, and the mapping in docker-compose.yml forwards
# to it. 5433 is the default here because a native PostgreSQL service commonly
# already holds 5432 on Windows, and two servers cannot share a port.
# Publishing the container beside it beats stopping the other service: "shut
# down your other database first" is not a setup anyone can reproduce.
#
# If you connect on the wrong one you will reach a real, working Postgres --
# just not this one. `select version();` is the cheap way to tell them apart.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
POSTGRES_HOST=localhost

# --- Kafka ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Not parameterised the way the Postgres port is, and deliberately so: the
# broker's KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS in docker-compose.yml also names 9092,
# because that is the address the broker hands back to clients after they
# connect. Changing the published port without changing the advertised one
# gives you a broker that accepts a connection and then redirects the client
# to a port nothing is listening on -- the single most confusing Kafka
# failure. Change both together, or neither. Nothing usually contends for 9092.
KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=localhost:9092
